VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OGW-044
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe psychological distress (panic attack, insomnia, somatic gastrointestinal symptoms) driven by an escalating, hostile workplace environment.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated acute psychiatric decompensation in a young female, presenting with severe hyperventilation, intractable insomnia for 4 days, and intractable vomiting secondary to severe stress. The patient explicitly linked the crisis to ongoing sexual harassment and retaliation at her workplace. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to provide acute medical stabilization (antiemetics/anxiolytics), document the somatic manifestations of the abuse, and establish immediate psychological safety before the morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I cannot stop throwing up, my heart is racing constantly, and I haven't slept in days because I am absolutely terrified to go back to my office on Monday.
History of Present Illness: Symptoms have escalated over the past several weeks, culminating in an acute crisis this weekend. The patient reports a 3-month history of unwelcome sexual advances and requests for sexual favors from a direct supervisor. She describes the creation of an intimidating, hostile, and offensive work environment. Recently, after formally rejecting the advances, she experienced disparate treatment regarding her employment (demotion of duties) and retaliation. This has caused a noticeable decline in her occupational performance. Tonight, the anticipatory anxiety triggered severe psychological distress, including a severe panic attack, profound insomnia, and somatic symptoms (intractable stress-induced vomiting and headaches). She reports intense feelings of isolation and marginalization.
Functional Status: Functionally paralyzed by severe acute stress; unable to eat, sleep, or leave the apartment.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of severe psychological trauma and occupational burnout; patient is isolated.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 mmHg | HR: 112 bpm | Temp: 36.7 C | RR: 24 bpm | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 60 Kg
General: Patient is acutely distressed, hyperventilating, tearful, and holding an emesis basin.
Physical Exam: Physical exam is largely unremarkable. Abdomen is soft, non-tender (ruling out acute organic pathology for the vomiting). Cardiovascular exam shows sinus tachycardia consistent with a severe panic state. Mental Status Exam reveals an extremely anxious, hypervigilant affect. Speech is rapid. Thought content is entirely focused on the workplace trauma and fear of the perpetrator. Insight is fully intact. No suicidal ideation reported.
Environmental Observations: Clean apartment, but patient appears unkempt, reflecting a few days of severe distress.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Stress Reaction / Severe Panic Attack (Secondary to documented workplace sexual harassment and retaliation) - Status: Acute psychological crisis
Differentials:

Generalized Anxiety Disorder - The symptoms are exceptionally acute and strictly tied to a specific external trauma (workplace environment), making an acute stress reaction more accurate.

Acute Gastroenteritis - Ruled out as the cause of vomiting given the absence of diarrhea, fever, and the clear, undeniable temporal link to the psychological panic regarding Monday's return to work.

Major Depressive Disorder - While depressive symptoms are present, the primary acute presentation tonight is massive sympathetic nervous system overdrive (panic).
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Severe psychosocial stressor)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is experiencing a severe acute stress reaction with pronounced somatic manifestations (tachycardia, vomiting, insomnia) directly triggered by a hostile and discriminatory workplace environment (diagnostic tetrad clearly met). The objective clinical assessment confirmed that the physical symptoms are psychogenic in origin, driven by massive sympathetic arousal. Urgent after-hours intervention was absolutely necessary to break the severe panic and vomiting cycle with parenteral medication, provide acute psychiatric stabilization, and critically, to provide an immediate medical certificate enforcing a leave of absence to instantly remove the anticipatory threat of returning to the hostile environment on Monday.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered intramuscular metoclopramide 10mg to immediately halt the stress-induced vomiting. Executed 10 minutes of guided grounding exercises, successfully reducing the respiratory rate to normal. Issued a formal medical certificate placing the patient on immediate "Stress Leave" for two weeks, explicitly stating she is medically unfit for work. Prescribed a short course of oral temazepam 10mg strictly for sleep induction over the next 3 days.
Positive Behavioral Change: To instantly reduce the immediate threat causing your panic, email the medical certificate to your HR department right now and turn off your work phone; executing this physical boundary will definitively prove to your brain that you are safe from that environment on Monday. To break the physical stress loop, force yourself to sip a high-calorie rehydration drink slowly over the next hour; realizing you can hold fluids down again will rebuild your confidence in your body's stability.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to specialized workplace psychological counseling services.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not applicable.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3 days for a psychological welfare check and to arrange a comprehensive psychological evaluation.

